WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.540
Omar manages a fictional office building.

2
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.170
On his screen, the building’s
digital model looks perfect.

3
00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.999
Every pump shows green.

4
00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:13.650
But down in the plant room, one pump
sounds wrong, and the room feels warm.

5
00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:16.340
A building information model can organise

6
00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:19.520
a building’s geometry
and asset information.

7
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.100
A model alone is not a
live operating-data feed.

8
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:28.000
A digital twin connects a model to
operating data, such as sensors,

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.100
the building management system
and maintenance records,

10
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.541
so the picture can
change with the building.

11
00:00:33.681 --> 00:00:35.221
In our fictional atrium,

12
00:00:35.221 --> 00:00:38.891
the pump in the model is linked
to a sensor on the real pump.

13
00:00:38.891 --> 00:00:41.455
Each reading arrives with a time stamp.

14
00:00:41.595 --> 00:00:43.040
Then the feed stops.

15
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.900
The model keeps showing the last
reading it received, so

16
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.697
the pump still looks fine.

17
00:00:47.837 --> 00:00:51.237
A connected model is only
as useful as its data.

18
00:00:51.237 --> 00:00:55.418
It has to be linked to the right
asset, and the data has to be fresh.

19
00:00:55.558 --> 00:00:59.588
A market report by Frost and
Sullivan, hosted by Autodesk,

20
00:00:59.588 --> 00:01:03.818
describes twins at different levels,
from gathering and describing data,

21
00:01:03.818 --> 00:01:07.236
to predicting, and at the
top, acting on their own.

22
00:01:07.376 --> 00:01:12.036
So not every twin predicts, controls,
or even updates continuously.

23
00:01:12.036 --> 00:01:15.286
It depends on how it was
built, and how it’s maintained.

24
00:01:15.426 --> 00:01:19.376
Omar checks the time stamp,
sees the reading is hours old,

25
00:01:19.376 --> 00:01:21.300
and sends someone to look at the pump.

26
00:01:21.356 --> 00:01:24.556
Before you rely on a building
model, ask four things.

27
00:01:24.556 --> 00:01:25.956
Which asset is this?

28
00:01:25.956 --> 00:01:27.210
What data feeds it?

29
00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.740
How fresh is that data?

30
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.166
And who verifies it?

31
00:01:30.306 --> 00:01:32.726
A beautiful model is a starting point.

32
00:01:32.726 --> 00:01:35.921
Fresh, verified data is
what makes it useful.

33
00:01:36.061 --> 00:01:37.531
Ahmadreza Samadi.

34
00:01:37.531 --> 00:01:39.468
More at a-samadi.com
